<h1>Handling Asynchronous Steps</h1>
<p>A step runs to completion before the flow continues. That is deliberate: the order of the steps in a flow is
	the order in which things happen, which is what keeps the log and the results tree readable. Some work does not
	fit that shape, though. A process that must keep running while other steps proceed, a background thread that
	collects measurements, a connection that must stay open: none of these can be finished inside one step.</p>
<p>Orcraft's answer is to split such work into two steps. A <strong>start</strong> step launches the work and
	returns at once, and a later <strong>read</strong> step collects its outcome. Between the two the flow does
	whatever must overlap, on the same endpoint or elsewhere. The thing that ties the two steps together is the
	object the start step created, a thread or a process handle, which it puts on its result. Such an object has no
	meaning off the machine that made it, so it does not travel to the controller. It stays on the endpoint, and the
	flow receives a <strong>reference</strong> to it. Handing that reference to a later step on the same endpoint
	gives that step the live object back. The framework reference calls these
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/steps/#remote-objects">non-transferable values</a>.</p>
<p>The <strong>Remote Thread Example</strong> test of the <strong>Endpoints</strong> group shows the pattern with a
	background thread that keeps solving mazes on an endpoint while the flow waits.</p>

<h2 id="the-three-steps">The start, wait and read steps</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_remote_thread_example/") }}
<p>The start step builds a small task record with a counter, a list of results, a lock and a stop event, starts a
	daemon thread that solves mazes until the event is set, and puts the record on its result:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
	self.result = StepStartMazeThreadResult()

	exe_folder = normalize_long_path(self.config.maze_executable_folder)
	width = int(self.config.width)
	height = int(self.config.height)

	task = {
		"count": 0,
		"results": [],
		"lock": threading.Lock(),
		"stop_event": threading.Event(),
	}

	def _worker() -> None:
		while not task["stop_event"].is_set():
			try:
				result = MazeApp(exe_folder, "solve", width=width, height=height).run_silent()
				if result.solved:
					with task["lock"]:
						task["count"] += 1
						task["results"].append(result)
			except Exception:
				pass

	thread = threading.Thread(target=_worker, daemon=True)
	task["thread"] = thread
	thread.start()

	Logger.log(
		f"Started background maze-generation thread (width={width}, height={height})",
		module_tag=False,
	)

	self.result.task = task
	self.result.status = OrcraftStatus.PASS
	return self.result') }}
<p><code>task</code> is a plain attribute of the result, not a declared result field, because it is meant for the
	flow and not for a column in the controller. It holds a thread and a lock, so it cannot be copied off the
	endpoint; what reaches the flow is a reference that stands for it.</p>
<p>The read step receives the task through a plain attribute of its config, which arrives as the live object
	again because the step runs on the same endpoint. It stops the thread, joins it, and turns the collected
	results into declared result fields:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
	self.result = StepReadMazeThreadResult()

	task = self.config.task
	if task is None:
		self.result.status = OrcraftStatus.ERROR
		self.result.error_description = "Task object is missing"
		return self.result

	task["stop_event"].set()

	task["thread"].join(timeout=float(self.config.join_timeout_sec))
	if task["thread"].is_alive():
		self.result.status = OrcraftStatus.ERROR
		self.result.error_description = "Background maze thread did not stop in time"
		return self.result

	with task["lock"]:
		count = int(task["count"])
		results = list(task["results"])

	step_counts = [result.steps for result in results]
	avg_steps = round(sum(step_counts) / len(step_counts), 1) if step_counts else 0

	self.result.mazes_generated = count
	self.result.avg_path_steps = avg_steps
	self.result.stopped = True') }}
<p>Between the two sits a wait step that only sleeps for the configured number of seconds. In a real test this is
	where the work that has to overlap with the thread would go.</p>

<h3 id="the-flow">The flow</h3>
<p>The flow runs the three steps on the test endpoint and passes the start step's <code>task</code> to the read
	step:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_flow(self) -> None:
	# Step 1: Start background maze-solving thread on test endpoint
	step_1_result = self.run_step(
		StepStartMazeThread,
		endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
		maze_executable_folder=self.config.maze_executable_folder,
		width=self.config.width,
		height=self.config.height,
	)

	if self.handle_errors(step_1_result, self.result):
		return

	# Step 2: Wait - thread keeps running, task object is held in step_1_result
	step_2_result = self.run_step(
		StepWait,
		endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
		wait_sec=self.config.wait_sec,
	)

	if self.handle_errors(step_2_result, self.result):
		self.run_step(
			StepReadMazeThread,
			endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
			task=step_1_result.task,
		)
		return

	# Step 3: Stop thread and collect results - using task from Step 1
	step_3_result = self.run_step(
		StepReadMazeThread,
		endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
		task=step_1_result.task,
	)

	if self.handle_errors(step_3_result, self.result):
		return

	self.result.mazes_generated = step_3_result.mazes_generated
	self.result.avg_path_steps = step_3_result.avg_path_steps') }}
<p>Note what happens when the wait step fails: the flow still runs the read step before returning, so the thread
	is stopped rather than left running on the endpoint. Whatever a start step launches, some step must always end
	it, on the failure path as well as on the happy one.</p>

