<p>Select the <strong>Remote Thread Example</strong> test, set its <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>, choose
	the Linux machine as the <strong>Test Endpoint</strong>, and raise <strong>Wait Sec</strong> from 2 to 5 to give
	the thread more time:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/asynchronous-steps/remote-thread-config.png", "Remote Thread Example run panel with Test Endpoint set to GMG-TEST-3, the Maze Executable Folder set, Width 5, Height 8 and Wait Sec changed to 5", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and
	confirm. When the run finishes, search the log for <code>Background maze thread stopped</code>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/asynchronous-steps/remote-thread-in-log.png", "Log of the Remote Thread Example run showing the Start Maze Thread step finishing in 0.000 s after logging that the background thread started, the Wait step waiting 5 seconds, and the Read Maze Thread step reporting the background thread stopped with 20 mazes generated", "large") }}
<p>The timings tell the story. <strong>Start Maze Thread</strong> finished in no time at all, because it only
	launched the thread. <strong>Wait</strong> took its five seconds, while the thread kept solving mazes on the
	endpoint. <strong>Read Maze Thread</strong> then stopped the thread and reported 20 mazes generated, and the
	log goes on to print each of them.</p>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/asynchronous-steps/remote-thread-details.png", "Remote Thread Example Details view with the three steps on GMG-TEST-3, the Start Maze Thread step elapsed 0.048 s, the Wait step 5.050 s and the Read Maze Thread step 0.241 s, and the run reporting 20 mazes generated with an average path of 22.8 steps", "large") }}
<p>The three steps ran on <strong>GMG-TEST-3</strong>, and the values the read step declared,
	<strong>Mazes Generated</strong> and <strong>Avg Path Steps</strong>, are carried up to the flow and the run as
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/result-fields/">result fields</a>. The thread itself never appears
	anywhere: it lived and died on the endpoint, and the flow only ever held a reference to it.</p>
<p>The same pattern serves a process started with <code>Popen</code>, an open socket, or any resource that must
	outlive one step. The reference is valid only on the endpoint that created it; passing it to a step on
	another machine, or to a local step, raises an error rather than silently copying something that cannot be
	copied.</p>

<p>The thread reference is a value the framework refuses to copy. The next page,
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/remote-type-fidelity/">Remote Type Fidelity</a>, is about the values it
	does copy, and how they keep their Python types on the way to an endpoint and back.</p>
